<!-- ─── Hero ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="top" style="padding:4.5rem 0 1rem">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Before &amp; after</p>
    <h1>Twelve jobs you currently do <span class="quiet">three times.</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">
      None of these are hard. That is the problem — each one is easy enough that you
      just write it again in the database, again in the API, and again in the browser,
      and then keep three copies in agreement forever. Nothing fails when they drift.
      A user finds out.
    </p>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1rem">
      The left column is a real stack, written the way you would actually write it.
      The right column is the same job when the schema is the thing everyone reads.
    </p>
    <div class="cluster" style="margin-top:1.75rem; gap:.75rem;">
      <a class="btn primary raised" href="/#start">Get started</a>
      <a class="btn outlined" href="/#code">See a whole app</a>
      <span class="badge warning" title="Working, but APIs may still move">Alpha</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ╔═══ BAND 1 ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗ -->
<div class="band" id="rules">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="n">Band 01</span>
    <span class="t">Rules that end up in three languages</span>
    <span class="s">The database, the handler, and the component all have an opinion.</span>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 1. ownership ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="row">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="job">
      <h2>Ownership visibility</h2>
      <p>“Only the owner can read a lead.” One sentence in the standup, three
         implementations in the repo.</p>
      <div class="cost">
        <div><span class="k">Before</span><span class="before">3 places · 3 languages</span></div>
        <div><span class="k">After</span><span class="after">1 line, on the Model</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class="colhead">Before <span class="count">— and nothing errors when they disagree</span></p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">Database</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S0}</pre></div>
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">API</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S1}</pre></div>
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">UI</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S2}</pre></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="after-col">
      <p class="colhead">After</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">db/schema.lite</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S3}</pre></div>
      </div>
      <p class="derived">
        The policy <strong>compiles into the query</strong>, so a route added next year
        is filtered too. The API returns <code>403</code> without a guard clause, and
        <code>leads.can('update', lead)</code> answers the same question in the browser —
        as an affordance, never a boundary.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 2. validation ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="row">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="job">
      <h2>Field validation</h2>
      <p>An email is an email in all three realms. You still declare it in all three,
         and the browser's copy is always the one that goes stale.</p>
      <div class="cost">
        <div><span class="k">Before</span><span class="before">3 declarations · kept in sync by hand</span></div>
        <div><span class="k">After</span><span class="after">1 declaration, read by all three</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class="colhead">Before</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">Database</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S4}</pre></div>
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">API</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S5}</pre></div>
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">UI</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S6}</pre></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="after-col">
      <p class="colhead">After</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">db/schema.lite</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S7}</pre></div>
      </div>
      <p class="derived">
        The migration takes the constraints. The API's <code>400</code>s are derived from
        the same rules. <code>leads.fields.email</code> is <strong>that rule in the
        browser</strong> — not a copy of it — and <code>leads.make()</code> renders an
        input the schema already describes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 3. permissions ───────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="row">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="job">
      <h2>Who may do what</h2>
      <p>Read is public, create needs a member, delete needs an admin. Now say that on
         every route, and again on every button.</p>
      <div class="cost">
        <div><span class="k">Before</span><span class="before">n routes × 4 verbs, plus the UI</span></div>
        <div><span class="k">After</span><span class="after">4 digits on the Model</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class="colhead">Before</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">API</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S8}</pre></div>
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">UI</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S9}</pre></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="after-col">
      <p class="colhead">After</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">db/schema.lite</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S10}</pre></div>
      </div>
      <p class="derived">
        Every service on that Model answers <code>401</code>/<code>403</code> at the right
        level without a guard, because the gate is <strong>under the data, not in front of
        the route</strong>. <code>leads.can('delete')</code> hides the button using the same
        four digits.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ╔═══ BAND 2 ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗ -->
<div class="band" id="wire">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="n">Band 02</span>
    <span class="t">The wire between them</span>
    <span class="s">Shapes that are nobody's job until they are wrong.</span>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 4. forms send strings ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="row">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="job">
      <h2>Forms send strings</h2>
      <p>Every input value is a string, including <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> and
         <code>type="number"</code>. And an empty box is <code>''</code>, which your
         nullable column does not consider empty.</p>
      <div class="cost">
        <div><span class="k">Before</span><span class="before">per-field coercion, per form</span></div>
        <div><span class="k">After</span><span class="after">two options, once</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class="colhead">Before</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">UI</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S11}</pre></div>
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">Database</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S12}</pre></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="after-col">
      <p class="colhead">After</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">web/src/routes/leads</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S13}</pre></div>
      </div>
      <p class="derived">
        Runs <strong>coerce → blankToNull → validate</strong>, so validation judges what
        is actually sent. <code>''</code> is never coerced to <code>0</code>, and a string
        that is not cleanly the target type is left alone for the server to reject.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 5. pagination & envelope ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="row">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="job">
      <h2>Pagination and the response shape</h2>
      <p>Every endpoint invents its own — a bare array here, <code>&#123; data, total &#125;</code>
         there — and every caller unwraps it differently.</p>
      <div class="cost">
        <div><span class="k">Before</span><span class="before">per-endpoint convention</span></div>
        <div><span class="k">After</span><span class="after">one envelope, one owner</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class="colhead">Before</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">API</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S14}</pre></div>
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">UI</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S15}</pre></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="after-col">
      <p class="colhead">After</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">Any service, no code</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S16}</pre></div>
      </div>
      <p class="derived">
        One envelope with <code>kind: 'single' | 'list'</code> as the discriminant — a list
        keeps it, a single unwraps. <strong>One module owns wrapping and unwrapping</strong>,
        so the browser never guesses.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 6. errors → status ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="row">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="job">
      <h2>Errors becoming status codes</h2>
      <p>Four libraries throw four shapes. Your error middleware grows an
         <code>instanceof</code> ladder, and anything it does not recognise is a 500 —
         including the 404 you meant.</p>
      <div class="cost">
        <div><span class="k">Before</span><span class="before">1 ladder, edited per library</span></div>
        <div><span class="k">After</span><span class="after">a numeric <code>status</code> on your error</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class="colhead">Before</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">API</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S17}</pre></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="after-col">
      <p class="colhead">After</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">Your package, anywhere</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S18}</pre></div>
      </div>
      <p class="derived">
        One boundary turns a thrown value into a status, and it recognises a numeric
        <code>status</code> without registration. <strong>Your errors need no dependency on
        the framework</strong> — and for a third-party error you cannot modify, register a
        mapper instead.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ╔═══ BAND 3 ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗ -->
<div class="band" id="change">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="n">Band 03</span>
    <span class="t">What one change costs</span>
    <span class="s">The real measure of a stack is what happens when a field moves.</span>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 7. add a field ───────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="row">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="job">
      <h2>Adding one field</h2>
      <p>A product manager adds “deal value”. Count the files.</p>
      <div class="cost">
        <div><span class="k">Before</span><span class="before">6 edits · 4 of them silent if missed</span></div>
        <div><span class="k">After</span><span class="after">1 edit · <code>fli db:migrate</code></span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class="colhead">Before <span class="count">— in order, and you must remember the order</span></p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">Six files</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S19}</pre></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="after-col">
      <p class="colhead">After</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">db/schema.lite</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S20}</pre></div>
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">Then</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S21}</pre></div>
      </div>
      <p class="derived">
        The migration, the <code>400</code>, the JSON Schema at <code>/manifest</code>, the
        TypeScript type, <code>leads.fields.value</code> and the input <code>make()</code>
        renders all move together — because <strong>none of them was ever a copy</strong>.
        There is no <code>generated/</code> directory to review or re-apply edits to.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ╔═══ BAND 4 ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗ -->
<div class="band" id="parts">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="n">Band 04</span>
    <span class="t">The parts you would otherwise go and install</span>
    <span class="s">Each one is a vendor, a config file and a new vocabulary. Here they are the framework.</span>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 8. realtime ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="row">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="job">
      <h2>Live updates after a mutation</h2>
      <p>Every handler that writes must also remember to announce. The one that forgets
         is the bug you cannot reproduce.</p>
      <div class="cost">
        <div><span class="k">Before</span><span class="before">an emit per handler · rooms by hand</span></div>
        <div><span class="k">After</span><span class="after">one declared channel</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class="colhead">Before</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">API</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S22}</pre></div>
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">UI</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S23}</pre></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="after-col">
      <p class="colhead">After</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">api/src/services/leads.service.ts</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S24}</pre></div>
      </div>
      <p class="derived">
        A mutation is announced in <strong>one place</strong> — the same call that fans out
        to the event bus and to the channel. A handler cannot forget to publish, because
        publishing is not something a handler does.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 9. jobs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="row">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="job">
      <h2>Background jobs and cron</h2>
      <p>A queue usually arrives with a Redis to run, a worker process to deploy, and a
         second place your app config lives.</p>
      <div class="cost">
        <div><span class="k">Before</span><span class="before">+1 service · +1 process · +1 config</span></div>
        <div><span class="k">After</span><span class="after">a table in the SQLite you already have</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class="colhead">Before</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">Infra</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S25}</pre></div>
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">API</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S26}</pre></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="after-col">
      <p class="colhead">After</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">jobs/send-email.job.ts</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S27}</pre></div>
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">Anywhere in the app</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S28}</pre></div>
      </div>
      <p class="derived">
        Same process, same file, same deploy. Retries, attempts and cron state are rows
        you can query — and job stats show up on <code>/metrics</code> because the queue is
        <strong>a plugin, not a neighbour</strong>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 10. notifications ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="row">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="job">
      <h2>Notifying a user</h2>
      <p>“Tell them in the app, and email them, and push it live.” Three code paths, and
         the copy is written three times so they drift immediately.</p>
      <div class="cost">
        <div><span class="k">Before</span><span class="before">3 paths · 3 copies of the wording</span></div>
        <div><span class="k">After</span><span class="after">1 class · 1 call</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class="colhead">Before</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">API</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S29}</pre></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="after-col">
      <p class="colhead">After</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">api/src/notifications/PaymentReceived.ts</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S30}</pre></div>
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">Anywhere</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S31}</pre></div>
      </div>
      <p class="derived">
        One class holds the message, and <code>via()</code> decides the channels
        per user. The in-app record, the live event and the email
        <strong>fan out from the same object</strong>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 11. email ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="row">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="job">
      <h2>Transactional email</h2>
      <p>Your app is components. Your email is a different templating language, a
         different component set, and a plain-text version nobody maintains.</p>
      <div class="cost">
        <div><span class="k">Before</span><span class="before">a second UI stack for email</span></div>
        <div><span class="k">After</span><span class="after">the same components, target <code>email</code></span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class="colhead">Before</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">Email</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S32}</pre></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="after-col">
      <p class="colhead">After</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">emails/Welcome.mesa</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S33}</pre></div>
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">Sending</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S34}</pre></div>
      </div>
      <p class="derived">
        Table-based markup and inlined CSS come out the other side, with the Outlook
        fallback handled and a <strong>real plain-text alternative</strong> derived from the
        same render — not a stripped copy of the HTML.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── 12. outbound ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="row">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="job">
      <h2>Calling someone else's API</h2>
      <p>Outbound <code>fetch</code> spreads until no one can answer “what does this
         service talk to, and with which credentials?”</p>
      <div class="cost">
        <div><span class="k">Before</span><span class="before">n call sites · n retry policies</span></div>
        <div><span class="k">After</span><span class="after">declared targets · one boundary</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class="colhead">Before</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">API, scattered</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S35}</pre></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="after-col">
      <p class="colhead">After</p>
      <div class="snips">
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">Declared once</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S36}</pre></div>
        <div class="snip"><p class="where">Anywhere</p><pre class="code">{@html sample.S37}</pre></div>
      </div>
      <p class="derived">
        Everything the app can reach is <strong>enumerable</strong>, credentials are
        references resolved at send time, and the result is
        <code>&#123; data &#125; | &#123; error &#125;</code> rather than a thrown fetch.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ─── Close ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section style="padding:4rem 0 1rem">
  <div class="container">
    <h1 style="max-width:24ch">None of this was hard. <span class="but">That was the problem.</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every line in the left column is code a good developer writes correctly. The cost
      was never difficulty — it was that the same fact lived in three places, and
      keeping them in agreement is work that never finishes and never appears on a
      roadmap.
    </p>
    <div class="cluster" style="margin-top:1.75rem; gap:.75rem;">
      <a class="btn primary raised" href="/#start">Get started</a>
      <a class="btn outlined" href="/#packages">See every package</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="/#idea">One seed, three realms</a>
      <span class="badge warning">Alpha</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- The page's own closing note. The site footer is the layout's. -->
<div class="container pagenote">
<span>Before columns are illustrative of a typical modern stack, written the way you'd actually write it.</span>
    <span>Styled entirely by <code>@frontierjs/css</code>. <a href="/">Home →</a></span>
</div>

<style>
h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.1rem); line-height: 1.06; letter-spacing: -.03em; margin: .35em 0 .3em; max-width: 20ch; }
  h1 .but { color: var(--color-primary); }
  h1 .quiet { color: var(--ink-mute); }
  .eyebrow { font-size: .78rem; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); margin: 0; }
  .lede { font-size: 1.06rem; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 60ch; line-height: 1.6; }

  /* ── band headings ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .band { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); background: var(--surface-sunken); padding: 1.1rem 0; margin-top: 3rem; }
  .band .container { display: flex; gap: 1rem; align-items: baseline; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .band .n { font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); }
  .band .t { font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -.01em; }
  .band .s { font-size: .86rem; color: var(--ink-mute); }

  /* ── the three-column row ──────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .row { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding: 2.25rem 0; }
  .row:first-of-type { border-top: 0; }
  .row .container { display: grid; gap: 1.5rem; grid-template-columns: minmax(0,1fr); align-items: start; }
  @media (min-width: 68rem) {
    .row .container { grid-template-columns: 15rem minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,1fr); gap: 2rem; }
  }

  .job h2 { font-size: 1.08rem; letter-spacing: -.01em; margin: 0 0 .45rem; }
  .job p { font-size: .87rem; color: var(--ink-mute); margin: 0; line-height: 1.55; }
  .cost { margin-top: .9rem; display: grid; gap: .3rem; font-size: .76rem; }
  .cost div { display: flex; gap: .5rem; align-items: baseline; }
  .cost .k { color: var(--ink-mute); min-width: 3.4rem; }
  .cost .before { color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .cost .after { color: var(--color-success); font-weight: 600; }

  .colhead { font-size: .7rem; letter-spacing: .14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); margin: 0 0 .55rem; display: flex; gap: .5rem; align-items: baseline; }
  .colhead .count { color: var(--ink-soft); letter-spacing: 0; text-transform: none; font-size: .74rem; }

  .snips { display: grid; gap: .55rem; }
  .snip { border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; background: var(--surface-sunken); }
  .snip .where { font-size: .68rem; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); padding: .38rem .8rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); background: var(--surface-raised); }
  .after-col .snip { border-color: var(--color-primary); }
  .after-col .snip .where { color: var(--color-primary); }

  /* No code palette and no block rules here. A sample is the design
     system's code block wrapping glow's own code element, and
     @frontierjs/css styles both — a rule here would be a second
     answer to what code looks like. */
  /* The one thing the design system cannot know: these sit in a dense
     grid, so they take the rung below the block default. */
  .code { font-size: var(--text-xs); padding: var(--space-md) var(--space-lg); }

  .derived { margin: .6rem 0 0; font-size: .8rem; color: var(--ink-mute); line-height: 1.55; }
  .derived strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
</style>
